     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is double the usual maximum for adults, which is 3,200 mg/day under supervision (2,400 mg/day is typical, and 1,200 mg/day for over-the-counter use). A single 1,600 mg dose is also above the normal single-dose ceiling of about 800 mg. At this level the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events goes up sharply.\n\n2. **You aren't a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed clinician who can verify the patient and the indication. Order entry should go through the patient's care team.\n\nIf this came from a prescriber, they should confirm the intended dose. It may be a typo, for example 400 mg or 800 mg per dose. A pharmacist can also help with verification. Once a clinician confirms a safe dose, I can enter the order."
      }
     ],
